  "worked_solution": "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\u2323$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\u2322$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($\u2323$) over the interval $(2,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/ece77074a3d712b76ed55150910382ed5c0bb1ddf7d565bc2d2200a72394c4b1.png)",
